To rewire your brain from “Story Thinking” to “State Thinking,” you must accept one thing:

Your brain naturally defaults to narratives, not state machines.

So if you want to think like an Elite problem solver, you need strict training constraints 
that force your brain to reason in terms of state, dependency, and transitions.

1) The “No-English” Variable Rule
          Never name variables based on the story

          Avoid 
              temp, flag, count, currentHolding
              These names are vague and unstable

          Use state-defined names instead
              min_so_far, max_ending_at_i, unmatched_open_brackets, visited_bitmask
              Precise names force precise thinking.

2) Write the State Schema Before Coding
          Before writing a loop, define the exact state being tracked
          If you cannot define the state cleanly, you are not ready to code.

3) Think in State Transitions
                Do not think in stories. 
                Think in deterministic transitions:
                            Next State=f(Current State,Input)
                Example
                    Instead of 
                          Add numbers… if negative reset…
                    Think 
                          max_ending_here = max(nums[i], max_ending_here + nums[i])
                          global_max = max(global_max, max_ending_here)
      
                Pure state transformation. No narrative. No ambiguity.

4) The “No-History” Rule
                Do not let your loop remember the past implicitly
                Your next step should depend only on
                              the current input
                              and the current state
                Think like a Markov process

5) Separate “State Update” from “Answer Capture”

6) Monotonic Structures Have One Truth
                A monotonic stack or queue is not “a push/pop story.
                It is a constraint engine
                Define the invariant:
                      The stack must remain strictly increasing
                Then aggressively enforce it
                You are not managing elements, You are protecting an invariant.

7) The “No-Run” Drill
                Stop relying on the compiler to debug your thinking
        
